  overview: "Posterior shoulder instability accounts for about 10% of shoulder instability, and acute traumatic posterior dislocation is rare, with an incidence of 1.1 per 100,000 person-years, about 20 times lower than anterior dislocation [r1]. Because it is uncommon, it is often misdiagnosed or diagnosed late: more than 60% of posterior dislocations were misdiagnosed initially in one review [r2], and up to 79% in some studies [r3]. The usual causes are blunt force or axial load through a flexed, adducted and internally rotated arm, repetitive microtrauma in contact and overhead athletes [r1,r4], and the forced muscle contraction of an epileptic seizure, electric shock or electroconvulsive therapy [r3].\n\nTwo clinical pictures need different thinking. An acute locked dislocation, typically after a seizure, shock or high-energy trauma, is an emergency: the arm is fixed in internal rotation and the humeral head is often impacted on the posterior glenoid rim (a reverse Hill–Sachs lesion) [r3,r2]. Recurrent posterior subluxation in an athlete, for example a lineman, weightlifter or thrower, usually comes from repeated posteriorly directed loads that injure the posteroinferior labrum and capsule [r4]. Risk factors for recurrent instability include age under 40 years at the first event, dislocation during a seizure, a large reverse Hill–Sachs lesion and glenoid retroversion [r1].\n\nNon-operative rehabilitation is the usual first step for recurrent subluxation, and it works for many people: in a cohort of 81 patients with a posterior clunk on the jerk test, 50 of 54 shoulders (93%) with a painless jerk test responded to rehabilitation after a mean of 4 months, compared with 5 of 35 (16%) with a painful jerk test [r5]. When rehabilitation fails, arthroscopic posterior capsulolabral stabilisation gave low recurrence (7.4%) and high satisfaction (90.9%) in a systematic review of 48 studies and 2307 shoulders, though the evidence is observational [r6]. Bone-based procedures for retroversion or bone loss have much less reliable results [r7,r8].",

